{% block title %}AC12.01 – Measuring and Analyzing Network Signals{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <div class="container">

        <h1>AC12.01 – Measuring and Analyzing Network Signals</h1>
        <p><strong>Objective:</strong> Analyze network exchanges and understand how protocols used in VoIP telephony work.</p>

        <hr>

        <h2>Reflective Analysis</h2>
        <p>
            During the VoIP telephony labs, I used an IPBX system with FreePBX and the 3CX Phone softphone
            to set up and test VoIP communications between multiple SIP endpoints.
            I learned how to analyze network exchanges using Wireshark by observing the different steps of a SIP call
            such as phone registration, call establishment, and call termination.
        </p>

        <p>
            I was able to identify the main SIP messages such as REGISTER, INVITE, RINGING, OK, and BYE,
            as well as RTP streams used to carry voice data.
            This activity helped me understand the difference between SIP signaling and RTP audio transport
            in a VoIP infrastructure.
        </p>

        <p>
            I also used tools such as ping, traceroute, and tshark to check network connectivity,
            diagnose communication issues, and capture network traffic directly from a Linux server.
            Finally, this work raised my awareness of security issues related to VoIP,
            especially the possibility of eavesdropping on RTP streams when they are not encrypted.
        </p>

        <h2>Evidence Documents</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Wireshark captures of SIP and RTP exchanges.</li>
            <li>SIP softphone configuration on 3CX Phone.</li>
            <li>Call tests between softphones and IPBX.</li>
            <li>Analysis of SIP / RTP network traffic.</li>
            <li>Ping and traceroute results.</li>
            <li>tshark captures performed on the IPBX server.</li>
            <li>SIP trunk and outbound route configuration.</li>
            <li>Setup of an Interactive Voice Response (IVR) system on FreePBX.</li>
        </ul>

